"Small seal script looks neat, with smooth, even lines, but it takes time to write. Xu Shen, a scholar of the Han dynasty, wrote that in the Qin dynasty the government offices and prisons had so much work that a new kind of writing appeared to make things quick and simple. That writing was clerical script. Clerical script changed the shapes of characters. Round turns became square corners. Long curves became straight lines. Characters that stood tall in seal script became flat and wide. This change is called libian (隸變), and after it, characters began to look like the ones we write today. The most famous clerical stroke is the long horizontal line. It starts with a silkworm head: the brush touches down moving backward, then turns and travels to the right, so the start looks round. It ends with a swallow tail: the brush presses down, then lifts as it flicks up to the right. A clerical character usually has only one swallow tail. Look at 三: only the long bottom line has a tail, and the two lines above end round. Later calligraphers summed up this habit in a saying: wild geese do not fly in pairs, and a silkworm head is not set down twice. Clerical script was the common script of the Han dynasty. People wrote it with a brush on narrow slips of bamboo and wood, and tied the slips together with cords to make a book. More than 10,000 Han dynasty slips were found near Juyan, on the northwest frontier, in 1930 and 1931. Today they are kept at Academia Sinica in Taipei. In this lesson, replay the three parts of the long horizontal stroke in 3D, watch seal script change into clerical script, and unroll a scroll of bamboo slips. Then drag the line between the two scripts, find the swallow tails, and trace the strokes yourself.": "441991728873",
